<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-derivativeApplications" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Applications of the Derivative</title>


  <introduction>
    <p> In the previous chapters we developed the rules and techniques for computing derivatives. However, the true 
      power of derivatives lies not just in calculating slopes, but in using those slopes to understand and solve 
      meaningful problems. Derivatives provide information about how quantities change, and that information allows us 
      to analyze the behavior of functions, make predictions, and determine optimal outcomes in many different contexts.
    </p>
    <p>
In this chapter we explore several important applications of derivatives. We begin by using derivatives to identify 
maximum and minimum values of functions and to understand the overall shape of graphs through curve sketching. These 
tools allow us to describe where a function increases or decreases, where it bends upward or downward, and how these 
features combine to form a complete picture of the graph.
</p>
    <p>
We then extend these ideas to a variety of practical settings. Derivatives help us solve optimization problems, 
analyze rates of change in related quantities, and interpret motion in physics. Along the way we develop important 
theoretical results such as the Mean Value Theorem, and we see how derivatives can be used both to approximate complicated 
functions and to evaluate difficult limits using L'Hôpital's Rule. Together, these topics illustrate how derivatives serve 
as one of the most powerful tools in calculus for understanding change.
    </p>
</introduction>

  
